92186050376 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428507 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1031 "The Characteristic Equation of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1033 1964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1034 1.1215558110164883 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "44312a5e-e7e0-507f-86f6-c3bcdecfd7fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050820 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050820 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050820 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050820 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050820 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050820 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050820 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050820 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428522 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050820 #edb/ref 1031 "Calculating the Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix With Distinct Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050820 #edb/ref 1033 1977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050820 #edb/ref 1034 0.640892872444543 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050820 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6902b215-0b25-5d7a-a72f-3376ac4e0554" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124341 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5504" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124341 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $A$ for which $\\lambda_1=-1,$ $\\lambda_2=1,$ $\\lambda_3=0$ are eigenvalues, and\n![](images/10/10c3a1128f78cd19b14ca29bda23bcd3c14a49a6bbf2608aa568f6f7c22aea9b.svg)\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n![](images/83/837556eba2375812c7d20c59533fc51f91716c6306254d2fc193b98315dd5663.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124341 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a285ae3e-4f06-5e09-a06f-b2620be99888" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124341 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-1},$ $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1},$ $\\lambda_3=0,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n![](images/20/20ee95979c2ae9a6f3eca54e65923f608ef569141c6ff1393ef53e99636dda1a.svg)\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-1},$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1},$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_3=0.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n![](images/13/13f61ba37464f828258713594c973c4ab7c5c1d6aa50c2b482dcc2b444013686.svg)\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n![](images/69/69506c47b7fce7fd91eee135299db2920598325d4eb28611d758aeb5e3815489.svg)\n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=0,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124342 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5505" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124342 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n![](images/3e/3e68233c9221816c1bd834d2260a162ec6d4cc5e063022c8b9e545948f703589.svg)\nDetermine whether $A=PDP^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124342 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3dd8d6e6-76a2-591c-90a5-86074539686b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124342 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $\\mathbb R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )([{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +2]-12) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -10) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(\\lambda -5)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=4,$ $\\lambda_2=5,$ and $\\lambda_3=-2.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n![](images/43/432aa48b4a3573c076a617dacc461cd5816c5279e79f9f14545609b0b4553f0c.svg)\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible  diagonal matrix is\n\n![](images/7c/7c7e458176039ee582b023ad53137c6adf5d66fdb7e17e2a62aedde6a160545a.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124343 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5506" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124343 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n![](images/95/95456befbc775262e58e48b5e32503b70e1a675d5c4b79911bfd3d526880d42e.svg)\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP,$ find the first column of the matrix $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124343 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b3307ae8-eb7d-533a-aef2-c7f50dea07e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124343 #edb/ref 1074 "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are  \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=-2, \\quad$ $\\lambda_2=-1, \\quad$ $\\lambda_3=2.$ \n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_1=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 14\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{8}{3}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_2=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_1=x_3.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/9a/9ac30b66a0fb5232b248e7ccf6d5b57267168e328387f233dfdb793fbb7ea287.svg)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_3=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/db/dbd8e786b6f8ffa50a35abc0136871f855f56fd4afff75c4ef6542dfa9b08f10.svg)\n\nFinally, ![](images/df/df31aa59467f4b7c37d2406d5ad6571a69baa6f9a7868f6bd868e45cf10c15aa.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124385 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7330" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124385 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0a/0acea11970e0a11fd3d6df09c46335ca747b58d82e6cf5a3c1bdfe941ccae242.svg) \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=2,$ $\\lambda_2=1,$ and $\\lambda_3=0.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D=P^{-1}AP.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124385 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8d2aff64-bf98-560b-867d-6b47ccc7dbe7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124385 #edb/ref 1074 "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=2,\\quad$ $\\lambda_2=1, \\quad$ $\\lambda_3=0.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](images/00/001911806161a251ef55b16759d586e6d6747a8d34bae00c07eb89c8179cde84.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](images/41/413d4dbc71e9f4059fc86d85806e009d5d41dfd8a87b9ea159badcc6003602e5.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_3=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](images/18/187539cd8867c5b8592cf3b6f928992f7be756c25a9a641741ba98150072f307.svg)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](images/a0/a09b2916af73b1d593ac237e5e895ff3d0c31d4978b66e4358dbd5f132547324.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124532 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124532 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/