,$ find the first column of the matrix $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125043 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b036f0f-ab37-53c9-a8c1-aa9fe52bf1e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125043 #edb/ref 1074 "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$\n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125108 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186382066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125108 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37843" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125108 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125108 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125108 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "be37aea9-9ebb-51c3-9045-5fceee548409" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125108 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ -1 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible matrix diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125423 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125423 #edb/ref 1061 20125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125423 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2b97df2a-2c81-5e5c-aae7-42dd0059d96e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125423 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125424 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125424 #edb/ref 1061 20126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125424 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c9398a9c-468d-5eda-8f0e-08b6ae582dd6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125424 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125425 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125425 #edb/ref 1061 20127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125425 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "72500366-9058-55a3-8409-3413cf056fad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125425 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125426 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125426 #edb/ref 1061 20128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125426 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "60b0ed38-7920-5718-bf00-7dd0beab7c34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125426 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125499 #edb/ref 1061 27129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125499 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f3ae6ecb-ff9c-5f85-9746-535278eb647f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125499 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125792 #edb/ref 1064 "An $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ is said to be diagonalizable if there exists a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that \n\n$$\nA=PDP^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nFor example, the matrix ![](images/81/810ae901dcd83f59297a122fbf20beb91e29374baff41c19bcacf40b3a7e7091.svg) is diagonalizable because\n![](images/2c/2c41221da0e4c0895928e65602e588f380452172e29d79f6e9f68ebb49e57807.svg)\n\nIn general, we have the following theorem:\n\n*An $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ is diagonalizable if and only if there exists a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that consists of eigenvectors of $A$.*\n\nSince eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are linearly independent, we can also  conclude the following fact:\n\n*If an $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ has $n$ *distinct* eigenvalues (that is, $n$ simple roots of the corresponding characteristic equation), then there exists a basis of eigenvectors. Thus, the matrix is diagonalizable.*\n\nLet's show you how to use the diagonalization theorem with an example. \n\nSuppose that $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-2},$ $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1},$ $\\lambda_3=-3$ are eigenvalues of a $3\\times 3$  matrix $A$, and that\n![](images/ec/ecc57b51a49c8d1d6fbadd69908dbf6f48803cd6ca1f72ad189a1e5410c5d71c.svg)\nare the respective eigenvectors. To find an invertible matrix $P$ and  a diagonal matrix $D$ such that $PDP^{-1} = A,$  we do the following: .\n\nFirst, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n![](images/4f/4f878ce6a4b6f770cd520e22a8e4a3c6f8b3012b7de446bd3f533e61bda85a13.svg)\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-2},$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1},$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_3=-3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n![](images/95/95c896911a6d54ea6385821846a3f0e4fc1a8f05f29e83b951ba270a3898938e.svg)\n\n**Note:** If the matrix $A$ does not have $n$ distinct eigenvalues, it is not always possible to diagonalize it. However, the algorithm to diagonalize any diagonalizable $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix is the same as the $3\\!\\times\\! 3$ case!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125792 #edb/ref 1065 3578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125792 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "aef11f46-9f49-598e-9d5b-938d54814c1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125792 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269486 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269486 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269486 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1ae4b6c0-9ac5-530e-a84b-630305f7709b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269863 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269863 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269863 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4210454b-1b57-5202-b24c-d9ba774f094c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270630 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270630 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270630 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9aa01c8c-fa5e-5db3-9890-98afc410a0cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270762 #edb/ref 1080 "$A$ is not diagonalizable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270762 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270762 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a6b21af7-648d-570d-aab4-7958bcae5273" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271467 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271467 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271467 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fb277ce2-2359-57d8-9955-89059a164bfc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271816 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271816 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271816 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271816 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271816 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186271467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271816 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271816 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271816 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186269486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271816 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a98d67e2-0ccf-58ae-aa10-005055ae0213" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272037 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272037 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140262" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272037 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272037 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=9.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272037 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d8196086-ee7b-5323-b641-b0ce439cc41f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272037 #edb/ref 1074 "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=9.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=9,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-9I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321263 #edb/ref 1080 "$A$ is not diagonalizable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321263 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321263 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ed43da4-d269-5604-bbf9-1927b6064761" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321271 #edb/ref 1080 "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321271 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321271 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0fe1b46a-c909-56fb-a2d0-1c237f2299b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321595 #edb/ref 1080 "D=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321595 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321595 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3a6e06cb-6b0a-5fad-bef9-74d23b2a7ea5" #edb/ref 175921860454