n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323946 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fe7fb554-d5d8-5ae2-835a-1ad3df46f8dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323946 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$\n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 7-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )+5) & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the quadratic polynomial $\\,{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\,$ we have that the discriminant $D<0.$ So, it doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable over $R.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323948 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323948 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37845" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323948 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323948 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323948 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "479c6325-498e-5e9a-912b-f302956edc64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323948 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable.  \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible matrix diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323949 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323949 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37849" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323949 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323949 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 16 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 20 & 6 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & a & 0 \\\\ 2 & b & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the value of $2a-b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323949 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1b47eadc-7593-50fb-a74a-94fb681ad288" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323949 #edb/ref 1074 "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=14,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=8.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{2}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 16 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 20 & 6 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 16 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 22 & 6 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 16 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 22 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 22 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -40 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+6{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd ones). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $2a-b=2\\cdot 1-0=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323950 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323950 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37850" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323950 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323950 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}15 & -4 & -16 \\\\ 40 & -5 & -40 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & ∗ & 0 \\\\ 4 & ∗ & -4 \\\\ -1 & ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the second column of  the matrix $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323950 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "11a60940-c504-5d41-97ac-a5508ab8072e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323950 #edb/ref 1074 "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=15,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ \n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{2}I & =[\\begin{aligned}15 & -4 & -16 \\\\ 40 & -5 & -40 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 & -16 \\\\ 40 & -4 & -40 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 & -16 & 0 \\\\ 40 & -4 & -40 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{16}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{4} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 40 & -4 & -40 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-40{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{4} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348563 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348563 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348563 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0d59528b-cdd3-5b86-8192-072c8f8d7878" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349352 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349352 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186349352 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "62e390e1-3e96-5e93-a534-8cd460604753" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350278 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350278 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350278 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d001947d-99b0-5aaf-8f74-6e8d15704c9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350366 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350366 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #e