95863 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395863 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ad98892c-f9b4-525c-8046-a410518643b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395864 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395864 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395864 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1dbfc55b-9986-5204-a717-051bef9ec970" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395871 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395871 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37846" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395871 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395871 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395871 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "21c29510-ffed-5744-82ad-55435a6f5d16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395871 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -9-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-16) & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -15) & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -5) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-9,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ Note that all $\\lambda $'s are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial.\n\nSince all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395872 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395872 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395872 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395872 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395872 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395872 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395872 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395872 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186395874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395872 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "72ff45fc-3165-5e0a-8ffa-750ac771413e" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
